00:01:27.010 --> 00:01:31.850
So maybe first we could talk about a little
bit the history of where did optogenetics

00:01:31.850 --> 00:01:37.340
come about from. Then you guys could talk
about using a little bit.

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:41.670
So interestingly, actually the history of
optogenetics can be traced back to an idea

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:48.680
that Francis Crick, who's famous for the double
helical structure of DNA first came up with.

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:53.891
He proposed that it would be really convenient
if we could activate neurons with light. He

00:01:53.891 --> 00:02:01.250
proposed that something like 30 years ago.
And it was people had tried different approaches

00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:07.869
and were developing different strategies for
trying to make it work and it was really the

00:02:07.869 --> 00:02:17.189
fortuitous recognition that a protein from
algae could be expressed in neurons and endow

00:02:17.189 --> 00:02:24.379
them with the ability to respond immediately
to to to light. And that happened I guess

00:02:24.379 --> 00:02:29.200
about a little over ten years ago and it transformed
how we do neuroscience.

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:34.200
When I started in neuroscience everything
in terms of trying to understand behavior

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:41.519
was correlational - we saw neurons fire and
we saw the behavior and we put them together

00:02:41.519 --> 00:02:47.829
and we of course all recognize that correlation
is not causality, but with without the ability

00:02:47.829 --> 00:02:52.879
to sort of dissect out the circuits that were
responsible for the behavior, all we could

00:02:52.879 --> 00:02:59.749
do is keep trying to find those associations.
And so that happened about I guess 2005 and

00:02:59.749 --> 00:03:05.519
within a couple of years lots of other, lots
of labs it happened. It was a paper from Karl

00:03:05.519 --> 00:03:11.159
Deisseroth and Ed Boyden. And within a couple
of years. Dozens and soon hundreds of labs

00:03:11.159 --> 00:03:14.669
were using it. So that's really the basic
history.

