was that instead of getting, making microscopy

00:37:25.630 --> 00:37:30.200
better so you could see smaller things. It
would be just as good if you could make the

00:37:30.200 --> 00:37:36.619
smaller things bigger. And so he developed
a technique that was really that was inspired

00:37:36.619 --> 00:37:43.500
by and actually uses literally the stuff that
they make diapers out of. And you know how

00:37:43.500 --> 00:37:48.691
you, for those of you who are familiar with
diapers you had liquid will say water to a

00:37:48.691 --> 00:37:58.750
diaper and it expands. And so he imbeds this
this this diaper stuff in between the bits

00:37:58.750 --> 00:38:07.420
of neuron and then binds every little protein
to a bit of it and then expands every protein

00:38:07.420 --> 00:38:12.950
isotropically in all directions. And so you
can get expansion factors that are two and

00:38:12.950 --> 00:38:18.510
four and now even twenty times the original.
So you can take a synapse which is below the

00:38:18.510 --> 00:38:25.800
resolution of light point below point two
microns and make it a three micron, five micron

00:38:25.800 --> 00:38:32.190
structure which we can easily resolve and
I think that that technique is really on the

00:38:32.190 --> 00:38:34.600
verge of transforming a lot of what we can
do.

00:38:34.600 --> 00:38:36.370
Can think, you merge that?

00:38:36.370 --> 00:38:42.350
So we are we are actively working with lab
to merge our approach. So all of this just

00:38:42.350 --> 00:38:49.710
to be clear is in fixed tissue and tissue
from the brain and after it's out of the skulls.

00:38:49.710 --> 00:38:55.640
Optogenetics is fantastic for working with
a live animal but then to figure out the circuitry

00:38:55.640 --> 00:39:00.380
of that animal that what you have to do is
take it out and then use sort of these methods

00:39:00.380 --> 00:39:10.370
so we are barcoding neurons in all the animals
you know regenerate to perform a task and

00:39:10.370 --> 00:39:17.770
then we expand the tissue and do the sequencing
in situ so we can actually sequence the barcodes

00:39:17.770 --> 00:39:22.910
while the bar codes are still inside the brain
and it gives us the spatial resolution to

00:39:22.910 --> 00:39:26.130
actually see which barcodes are at a synapse
and actually figure out which neurons are

00:39:26.130 --> 00:39:32.070
connected to each other neurons. So there
are some engineering issues, throughputs,

00:39:32.070 --> 00:39:36.840
throughput issues but conceptually we can
expand an entire brain and figure out the

00:39:36.840 --> 00:39:42.260
wiring diagram of an entire brain at synoptic
resolution. And while we're at it we can also

00:39:42.260 --> 00:39:49.470
figure out which genes are being expressed.
So this is, you know, one of one of the people

00:39:49.470 --> 00:39:54.410
in Ed’s lab calls it the Rosetta project
because basically we can figure out everything

00:39:54.410 --> 00:40:01.670
about a brain all the way from activity while
the animal is performing a task, the behavior.

00:40:01.670 --> 00:40:05.550
And then the circuitry and gene expression
that cause those things so that that's where

00:40:05.550 --> 00:40:07.790
we're hoping to go with them.

00:40:07.790 --> 00:40:13.450
That's pretty amazing to think about. So but
as you say that's finding out everything about

00:40:13.450 --> 00:40:14.450
a mouse brain.

00:40:14.450 --> 00:40:15.450
Yeah.

00:40:15.450 --> 00:40:19.691
So we're talking a lot about you know mouse
mice have human behaviors and things. You

00:40:19.691 --> 00:40:25.670
know, Elizabeth you talk a little bit that
you have done a very strong proponent of that

00:40:25.670 --> 00:40:32.290
we've already got great human imaging techniques
that you're using. But also in humans in trying

00:40:32.290 --> 00:40:38.190
to trying to study the differences and similarities
and what we can gain more out of our MRI techniques

00:40:38.190 --> 00:40:43.130
we already have and how you're going about
that and helping translate ideas.

00:40:43.130 --> 00:40:52.390
Sure. So, so I touched on this a little bit
before but you know anybody here had an MRI

00:40:52.390 --> 00:40:59.660
scan? Have you ever volunteered for a functional
MRI scan where they showed you how to do tasks

00:40:59.660 --> 00:41:05.610
and things like this? OK. So I've done lots
of them, I fall asleep I'm terrible subject.

00:41:05.610 --> 00:41:11.770
So we, so you go into an MRI scanner it makes
this beautiful image of your brain. And what

00:41:11.770 --> 00:41:15.440
they discovered about 30 years ago now is
if you actually took a sequence of images

00:41:15.440 --> 00:41:20.030
over time that you could see small changes
in the signal in different regions of the

00:41:20.030 --> 00:41:24.580
brain while you were doing things so like
if you actually moved your hand you could

00:41:24.580 --> 00:41:29.050
find a region of the brain that would have
some sort of correlated change in the MRI

00:41:29.050 --> 00:41:33.900
signal while you were doing that task and
we call that BOLD, signal the blood oxygen

00:41:33.900 --> 00:41:38.690
level dependent signal. And it's what generates
those beautiful movies images that you see

00:41:38.690 --> 00:41:43.140
on the front of the New York Times saying
this is the area of the human brain that reacts

00:41:43.140 --> 00:41:44.570
when you see a picture of a puppy or something.

00:41:44.570 --> 00:41:45.800
The puppy area

00:41:45.800 --> 00:41:53.470
And so you know I did my I did my postdoc
up in Boston in one of the centers that actually

00:41:53.470 --> 00:41:58.210
developed this method. And so right now you
know this is the best method that we have

00:41:58.210 --> 00:42:04.100
for looking at the human brain. It's noninvasive,
any one of you can go sit and do one of these

00:42:04.100 --> 00:42:09.710
studies and pretend to be a mouse, you know
tapping your fingers or being shown pictures.

00:42:09.710 --> 00:42:15.230
And, and it does a pretty good job it points
to this area here, this something happened

00:42:15.230 --> 00:42:21.140
here. Right? The problem is that that signal
actually comes about because there was a change

00:42:21.140 --> 00:42:26.619
in blood flow in that area. So there's not
really a change in the MRI signal when a neuron

00:42:26.619 --> 00:42:32.280
fires but there's a change in the magnetic
properties of blood when it becomes oxygenated

00:42:32.280 --> 00:42:36.510
or deoxygenated. And so that little signal
change that you see is actually coming from

00:42:36.510 --> 00:42:42.160
a change in blood flow to the area. And that's
why it gets a little bit confusing because

00:42:42.160 --> 00:42:45.530
it's a little slower than the neural activity
because it takes a while for blood flow to

00:42:45.530 --> 00:42:47.859
change after neurons fire.

00:42:47.859 --> 00:42:52.800
And so I've spent, in addition to the other
work I've done, I spent a long time studying

00:42:52.800 --> 00:42:57.580
this the relationship between neural activity
in the brain and the blood flow activity that

00:42:57.580 --> 00:43:03.910
that kind of accompanies it. Partly initially
to help with understanding those fMRI images

00:43:03.910 --> 00:43:09.540
better, functional MRI because when you're
seeing blood. We wanted to understand what

00:43:09.540 --> 00:43:15.240
that meant in terms of neurons I could talk
about this all day but it CT cause us to have

00:43:15.240 --> 00:43:19.750
to develop a lot of imaging methods to go
in and use microscopes to simultaneously look

00:43:19.750 --> 00:43:22.530
at neurons firing and blood vessels next to
them.

00:43:22.530 --> 00:43:29.140
And I got very interested in it from the standpoint
of it's actually quite surprising that every

00:43:29.140 --> 00:43:34.760
time or region of your brain has some neural
activity that there's actually a big increase

00:43:34.760 --> 00:43:39.750
in blood flow in that area and that has to
happen for a reason and that has to be an

00:43:39.750 --> 00:43:44.470
important part of brain health. And so now
my work has sort of moved towards not just

00:43:44.470 --> 00:43:49.960
trying to understand the fMRI signal better
but also recognizing that there might be situations

00:43:49.960 --> 00:43:53.940
in which that relationship between neural
activity and blood flow gets disrupted. And

00:43:53.940 --> 00:43:59.030
if every time you're hungry you try to eat
a sandwich but you miss something's going

00:43:59.030 --> 00:44:04.430
to happen you're going to lose weight you're
not going to perform as well. And so, what,

00:44:04.430 --> 00:44:07.421
all that stuff I showed you with the coffee
and the ketamine I mean the really the core

00:44:07.421 --> 00:44:12.670
of that study in the beginning was looking
at how that neural activity actually was coupled

00:44:12.670 --> 00:44:14.390
to blood flow changes.

00:44:14.390 --> 00:44:18.970
And we've got ways now we actually can give
drugs that disrupt the relationship between

00:44:18.970 --> 00:44:23.060
neural activity and blood flow and we can
then see what effects do they actually have

00:44:23.060 --> 00:44:27.210
in real time on neural activity, what effects
they have on behavior. So we're training the

00:44:27.210 --> 00:44:31.560
animals to sit and actually push a lever,
this is one of Nick's other projects right

00:44:31.560 --> 00:44:35.790
,there were we're try and get them to push
a lever disrupt this blood flow relationship

00:44:35.790 --> 00:44:41.320
and see whether they actually become slower
at pushing the lever simply because this is

00:44:41.320 --> 00:44:45.720
just another aspect of the brain that we really
don't understand.

00:44:45.720 --> 00:44:52.000
Stepping back if we can get a better handle
on this relationship between blood flow in

00:44:52.000 --> 00:44:58.130
your neural activity, it gives us a way to
really better use functional MRI and it has,

00:44:58.130 --> 00:45:04.860
this method has kind of an image problem,
right? We all agree. Because it's not so clear

00:45:04.860 --> 00:45:09.550
what it means. And in fact when you do it
in someone who just had a stroke you might

00:45:09.550 --> 00:45:14.350
see huge activity in an area where they're
paralyzed or you might see no activity at

00:45:14.350 --> 00:45:20.651
all and they go on to be, to have a great
recovery. And so sort of my mission is to,

00:45:20.651 --> 00:45:26.200
to use these methods more in the mice where
we can do this very detailed imaging but to

00:45:26.200 --> 00:45:30.450
try to disambiguate all of this. Try to find
is there an early signature of Alzheimer's.

00:45:30.450 --> 00:45:34.960
Is there something that happens in stroke
that we can actually understand in terms of

00:45:34.960 --> 00:45:40.960
what it's going to look like in MRI and then
can we use fMRI in a much more guided way

00:45:40.960 --> 00:45:46.790
to get to those conditions in human brain.
So sounds a little circuitous but it keeps

00:45:46.790 --> 00:45:47.790
me busy.

00:45:47.790 --> 00:45:51.802
I think we all agree it's something there's
a huge need for, to understand what is it

