00:46:10.320 --> 00:46:17.340
in my lab the opportunity to potentially use
brain imaging to rethink the way we diagnose

00:46:17.340 --> 00:46:21.670
psychiatry, if you make diagnoses in psychiatry.
If you'll bear with me for like two minutes

00:46:21.670 --> 00:46:27.310
I'll tell you an interesting historical like
little background story. So our DSM, this

00:46:27.310 --> 00:46:31.720
book that we use for diagnosis is kind of
seeped into the public lexicon right, you

00:46:31.720 --> 00:46:36.570
read about in the Times a lot of people are
familiar with it. And the whole way we diagnose

00:46:36.570 --> 00:46:42.630
mental illness is really not very old, it
was completely changed in 1980. I was born

00:46:42.630 --> 00:46:49.090
in 1980. You can't say that about very many
other areas of medicine. And yet it's the

00:46:49.090 --> 00:46:54.180
truth. Everything we do in psychiatry is quite
different from before 1980, due in part to

00:46:54.180 --> 00:46:58.750
this kind of controversial study that was
published in Science seven years before that

00:46:58.750 --> 00:47:04.610
just showed essentially that are our diagnostic
system at that time was not very reproducible

00:47:04.610 --> 00:47:09.270
and very prone to bias. So social psychologist
at Stanford very cleverly designed a set of

00:47:09.270 --> 00:47:14.450
experiments where he sent his investigators
into hospitals around the country presenting

00:47:14.450 --> 00:47:20.850
with a fake symptom. They said I'm experiencing
voices saying the words ‘empty’, ‘hollow’

00:47:20.850 --> 00:47:25.730
or ‘thud’ and otherwise they were supposed
to answer all questions honestly truthfully

00:47:25.730 --> 00:47:29.770
and and the results they just followed them
and the results were really shocking to everybody.

00:47:29.770 --> 00:47:36.890
They got different diagnoses in different
places and later work showed that your socioeconomic

00:47:36.890 --> 00:47:42.580
background and your ethnic background were
could bias the way people assigned diagnoses

00:47:42.580 --> 00:47:50.080
to you. And, and it was really hard to detect
that these were fake patients basically that's,

00:47:50.080 --> 00:47:52.910
that's what he showed you could bias people's
judgment in that way.

00:47:52.910 --> 00:47:57.390
And then in the second part of this study,
and this is kind of the clincher, in the second

00:47:57.390 --> 00:48:03.650
part of the study the lead of this study had
met the director of a famous hospital at the

00:48:03.650 --> 00:48:09.119
time and he said you know this is this is
an anomaly what you found is not representative

00:48:09.119 --> 00:48:13.550
of the way we do business. Come to my hospital
see if you can see if you'll get different

00:48:13.550 --> 00:48:17.630
results. And so they agreed he would send
an unknown number of people into his hospital

00:48:17.630 --> 00:48:23.670
over the next few months and during that time
their job was to figure out who is a fake

00:48:23.670 --> 00:48:27.930
patient who is a real patient. Twenty percent
of people were identified as definite impostures

00:48:27.930 --> 00:48:33.490
and other twenty percent were identified as
probable impostures. So fully two out of every

00:48:33.490 --> 00:48:38.180
five people who came to the hospital had,
were thought to be possible impostures. And

00:48:38.180 --> 00:48:42.920
the truth was that he hadn't sent any impostors
and that they were all real patients. So you

00:48:42.920 --> 00:48:45.870
could bias people's judgments in both ways.

00:48:45.870 --> 00:48:52.260
And so getting to that point here, the DSM3
which was released in 1980 and very similar

00:48:52.260 --> 00:48:57.080
to the one we use today was really designed
to lump lots of people together with very

00:48:57.080 --> 00:49:02.530
different problems in big diagnostic categories
that we could all agree on. And it did a really

00:49:02.530 --> 00:49:07.600
good job of that. The diagnoses are much more
reproducible than they used to be, less prone

00:49:07.600 --> 00:49:11.750
to bias. But the problem is they don't correspond
to biology very well.

00:49:11.750 --> 00:49:17.660
So, depression is a great example of that.
You have this like choose five or more of

00:49:17.660 --> 00:49:23.140
nine symptoms. So there's at least two hundred
fifty six ways you can be depressed. And some

00:49:23.140 --> 00:49:27.800
of them are almost opposites of one another
like sleeping nineteen hours a day, can't

00:49:27.800 --> 00:49:34.850
get out of bed. Not enjoying any activities,
very slowed, gained a lot of weight. That

00:49:34.850 --> 00:49:40.140
person is depressed. So is a person who's
sleeping three hours a day anxious, weight

00:49:40.140 --> 00:49:44.800
loss, almost the opposite. And they get the
same diagnoses but they probably don't have

00:49:44.800 --> 00:49:48.950
the same biological problem and it's a miracle
really that treatments work as well as they

00:49:48.950 --> 00:49:53.350
do in these in these very different people.
So what we're trying to do with fMRI is figure

00:49:53.350 --> 00:49:59.510
